   331	\subsection{Potential Function.} 
   332	To incorporate the derived contribution scores without biasing the optimization objective, we adopt potential-based reward shaping (PBRS) \cite{ng1999policy}. This is crucial because the contribution scores evolve during training. Prior work shows that PBRS preserves policy invariance and Nash equilibria even under dynamic shaping \cite{devlin2012dynamic}, and can safely embed arbitrary rewards by expressing them as dynamic potentials \cite{harutyunyan2015expressing}.
   333	%
   334	
   335	%
